add('11',6,4.5,'Algebra','Inequalities|Cauchy–Schwarz|Equality cases',r'Positive reals $a,b,c$ satisfy $a+b+c=1$. Prove that $\frac a{2a+1}+\frac b{3b+1}+\frac c{6c+1}\le\frac12$, and determine when equality holds.',r'''For any $u>0$, $(u-1)^2\ge0$ gives $u/(u+1)\le(u+1)/4$. Apply this with $u=2a,3b,6c$ and divide by 2,3,6, respectively:
$$\frac a{2a+1}\le\frac{2a+1}{8},\quad \frac b{3b+1}\le\frac{3b+1}{12},\quad \frac c{6c+1}\le\frac{6c+1}{24}.$$
Their right-hand sides sum to
$$\frac{a+b+c}{4}+\frac18+\frac1{12}+\frac1{24}=\frac12.$$
Equality holds precisely when $2a=3b=6c=1$, that is $(a,b,c)=(1/2,1/3,1/6)$.''','The unequal denominators obscure the equality point. A common tangent-line bound for u/(u+1), scaled by 2,3,6, makes all variable coefficients equal so that the condition a+b+c=1 can be used sharply.')
add('12',7,6,'Geometry','Incentre|Arc midpoints|Similarity|Point reflection',r'In triangle $ABC$ with incentre $I$, line $AI$ meets the circumcircle again at $M$, and $N$ is opposite $M$ on that circle. Line $NI$ meets the circle again at $X$. Let $D$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $I$ to $BC$, and suppose $\angle DXI=\angle IXA$. Prove that the reflection of $I$ in the point $X$ lies on the circumcircle of $ADI$.',None,'The arc-midpoint diameter MN is parallel to ID. Using this together with the supplied angle equality yields two linked similarities, first IXD with AXI and then the reflected configuration with AID. The stated extra angle condition removes a harder isogonal-geometry lemma.')
add('12',8,5.5,'Algebra','Functional equations|Multiplicativity|Primes|Injectivity',r'A function $f:\mathbb Z_{>0}\to\mathbb Z_{>0}$ satisfies $f(f(m)f(n))=mn$ for all positive integers $m,n$. Prove that $f(p)$ is prime whenever $p$ is prime.',r'''Putting $m=1$ gives $f(f(1)f(n))=n$, which proves both surjectivity and injectivity. Write $c=f(1)$ and choose $a$ with $f(a)=1$. The equation at $(1,1)$ gives $f(c^2)=1=f(a)$, so injectivity implies $a=c^2$. At $(a,a)$ it gives $c=a^2=c^4$. As $c$ is a positive integer, $c=1$.

It follows that $f(f(n))=n$. Apply $f$ to the original equation to obtain
$$f(mn)=f(m)f(n).$$
If $f(p)=uv$ with $u,v>1$, then
$$p=f(f(p))=f(u)f(v).$$
Injectivity and $f(1)=1$ imply $f(u),f(v)>1$, contradicting the primality of $p$. Hence $f(p)$ is prime.''','The core is forcing f(1)=1 rather than assuming it. Surjectivity supplies a preimage of 1, injectivity links it to f(1)², and a second substitution closes the loop. Involution and multiplicativity then transfer primality.')
